  "text": "We measured how quickly slow protons (energies between about 50 and 400 keV) lose energy when they travel through thin sheets of several metals, including a light metal, an intermediate one, and three heavier ones. The data match earlier results obtained at higher proton speeds (above 400 keV) wherever such data exist. The metal foils were made using a vapor‑deposition method, which we consider more reliable than the usual mechanically worked foils. Our findings show that the stopping power is roughly 15 % higher for the heaviest metal and about 7 % lower for the lighter metal compared with earlier published values. When we compare the results with theoretical predictions, the low‑speed theory agrees well, while the high‑speed model overestimates the stopping by about 10 %. We argue that this mismatch cannot be explained by assuming the proton carries less than a full elementary charge due to electron‑capture processes."
